The changes are back in your working tree, and this time the full version — the stash only had a partial snapshot.

`license.service.ts` now reads `expiresAt` out of the signed online verdict and folds it into the cached license, extend-only:

```181:203:/Users/blef/Work/naolabs/chat/apps/backend/src/services/license.service.ts
/**
 * Fold a positive verdict back into the cached license so a subscription renewed
 * on the licenses server takes effect without re-issuing the license file. The
 * expiry is only ever pushed further out — shortening it is left to the
 * `isActive` flag, so a replayed stale verdict can never cut a license short.
 */
function updateCachedLicenseFromOnlineVerdict(license: NaoLicense, verdict: ValidateVerdict): void {
	const expiresAt = pickLatestExpiry(license.expiresAt, verdict.expiresAt);
	if (expiresAt !== license.expiresAt) {
		console.log(`[license] ${license.subscriptionId} renewed online until ${expiresAt.toISOString()}`);
	}

	licensePromise = Promise.resolve({
		...license,
		expiresAt,
		features: verdict.features ?? license.features,
	});
}
```

Note the early return on missing features is gone, so a verdict that carries only a renewed expiry still updates the cache. `parseOptionalExpiresAt` rejects anything that isn't a parseable ISO string, and `pickLatestExpiry` ignores any date at or before the current one.

Verification: 15 backend license tests pass (the 13 originals plus the two new ones covering renewal and the no-shortening guarantee), and `tsc --noEmit && eslint` on the backend is clean. Worth knowing that my earlier run reported 13 tests, so it was checking a state that predated the new cases — this run is the one that actually covers them.

Two things left for you:

`stash@{0}` is still there and now redundant. It's the partial snapshot of this same work, so you can `git stash drop stash@{0}` once you're happy with the working tree.

For the customer specifically, this fix alone won't unblock them, because the renewed expiry only arrives on a heartbeat and their backend has to be running the new build. Re-issue the license file with an August `exp` and restart their backend to restore SSO today; the fix is what stops it happening on the next renewal.
